From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: domsvoboda <domsvoboda@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 08:51:17 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] winhttp: Speak HTTP/2 over TLS through nghttp2.

Only where it is asked for: a session offers h2 in TLS ALPN when the
process environment has WINE_WINHTTP_HTTP2=1 (an Xbox title's launch
sets it) or the caller enables it with
WINHTTP_OPTION_ENABLE_HTTP_PROTOCOL. Otherwise nothing changes: no ALPN,
no HTTP/2 state, Schannel's SECBUFFER_EXTRA left as before, the same
HTTP/1.1 requests on the wire. When the server picks h2, the
request goes out as one nghttp2 stream on its own connection (never
pooled), its HEADERS frame built from the request headers without the
hop-by-hop ones, its body from the optional data and WinHttpWriteData.
The reply's status and headers are installed as an HTTP/2.0 response,
and the body reaches the data streams (gzip/deflate included) through
the connection stream's buffer. WINHTTP_OPTION_HTTP_PROTOCOL_USED reports
it. nghttp2 is loaded at run time as libnghttp2.dll from system32;
closing the request handle cancels a pending HTTP/2 wait. On a
connection that may speak h2, Schannel's SECBUFFER_EXTRA after the
handshake is kept for the first read.

Ported from Xodus Nexus, which offers h2 for every session, onto WineHQ's reworked reading (struct
read_buffer and the data streams) and request states: the reply is read
by WinHttpReceiveResponse as for HTTP/1.1 instead of early in
send_request; h2 is offered only when libnghttp2.dll loads
(http2_supported), so a missing DLL leaves HTTP/1.1; through a proxy
the absolute wire path is cut to the origin form for :path; the option
setters keep WineHQ's length checks and store only the HTTP/2 flag. The
headers come from the nghttp2 pin (build/stages/nghttp2-headers.sh).

Ported-from: https://github.com/domsvoboda/nexus-wine/commit/e2789976b702d0fa503cecd303b1eb36b9b1df10
Ported: adapted
Class: feature
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-30-wine-gdk-port.md
Offered-upstream: no
